  {
   "slug": "speech-825994",
   "speaker": "Mitch Fifield",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2013-12-02",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The government moved to repeal the carbon tax effective 1 July 2014, claiming it would lower the Consumer Price Index by 0.7 percentage points, reduce average electricity prices by nine per cent and gas prices by seven per cent, and cut household costs by $550 in the first year. The bill also repeals the second round of personal income tax cuts scheduled for 1 July 2015, keeping the tax-free threshold at $18,200, the 32.5 per cent marginal rate, and the $445 low-income tax offset, legislating a $1.5 billion saving."
